<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harbin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harbin is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilongjiang">Heilongjiang</a> province in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhua-river">Songhua River</a>. It grew from a small settlement after 1898, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> made it the headquarters of the Chinese Eastern Railway, and became a cosmopolitan city with a large Russian population. Under Japanese rule it was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>, and the notorious Unit 731 carried out biological warfare experiments on prisoners at nearby Pingfang.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harbin.jpg" alt="Harbin" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:26935-Harbin_(29661238117).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Harbin lies in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilongjiang">Heilongjiang</a> on the south bank of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhua-river">Songhua River</a>, at the south-eastern edge of the Songnen Plain. The terrain is generally flat and low. It is the northernmost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s major cities and has long, dry and freezing winters, which have earned it the nickname Ice City. The city government says the name means 'swan' in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a>, while other sources derive it from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> word meaning 'a place for drying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fishing-net">fishing nets</a>'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human settlement in the area dates from at least 2200 BCE. Wanyan Aguda, founder of the Jin dynasty, who reigned from 1115 to 1123, came from the Jurchen Wanyan tribes living near the Ashi River. In 1115 he established the Jin capital, Shangjing Huiningfu, in what is now Acheng District of Harbin. Wanyan Liang moved the capital to Yanjing, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in 1153, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shizong-of-jin">Wanyan Yong</a> restored the city as a secondary capital in 1173.</p><p>The modern city dates from 1898, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> built the Chinese Eastern Railway. The Polish engineer Adam Szydłowski drew plans for the city, and the Russians made it the base of their administration of the railway. The settlement grew into a city within five years, with settlers from across the Russian Empire, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>. In the 1920s it was considered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s fashion capital.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukden-incident">Mukden Incident</a> of September 1931, and Japanese forces occupied Harbin within hours. From 1932 until 1945 it was the largest city of the puppet state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>. The Soviet Army took the city on 20 August 1945 and handed it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> in April 1946. Most of the European community left in the early 1950s, so that by 1964 the Russian population was reduced to 450.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Harbin is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilongjiang">Heilongjiang</a>, with a metropolitan population of 10,009,854 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its Russian and European legacy survives in Central Street and in Saint Sophia Cathedral. UNESCO named Harbin a City of Music in 2010, and the city is known for its winter ice sculpture festival.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harbin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the eleventh century the Wanyan clan, based on the Anchuhu river near modern <b>Harbin</b>, united many Jurchen tribes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The disbanded troops joined the &quot;righteous armies&quot;, peasant militias that fought the Japanese until a scorched-earth campaign drove them into Manchuria and Russia, and on 26 October 1909 Ahn Jung-geun assassinated Ito Hirobumi in <b>Harbin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/korea-under-japanese-rule">Korea under Japanese rule · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kwantung Army also ran Unit 731, the biological and chemical warfare unit near <b>Harbin</b>, at Beiyinhe from 1932 to 1936 and then at Pingfan, whose doctors vivisected Chinese, Russian and Korean victims, sometimes without anaesthesia, and used the Russian Fascist Party to kidnap 'unreliable' Russians as subjects.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It was written in the spring of 1964 by two artists of the <b>Harbin</b> Opera House, the composer Wang Shuangyin and the lyricist Yu Wen (Li Yuwen), at a time of a nationwide campaign to study Mao Zedong's writings; its first title was &quot;Making Revolution Depends on Mao…</blockquote><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</a></li><li><blockquote>The territory was linked to <b>Harbin</b> by the southern branch of the Chinese Eastern Railway.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwantung-oblast">Kwantung Oblast</a></li></ul></section>
